@database "agent_editor"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: agent_editor.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Agent Editor"
@{h1}Agent Editor

Agents are entries in the YAML files of the config tree: a model chain,
the tools they may use, a prompt, hooks, limits. The @{b}Agent Editor@{ub}
panel lists every agent, shows what it sets itself and what it inherits,
and lets an admin change it in a form. A save writes only the agent's own
entry back into the file it came from -- comments, layout and the rest of
the file stay as they are -- after showing the change as a diff. New agents
get a file of their own; the panel also says which agents run with older
settings than the files hold.

The plugin gives agents no tool and no hook: it is a panel for people.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Agent Editor with an agent open on its General tab"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The panel: list and head  " link panel}
  @{"  The tabs  " link tabs}
  @{"  The Tools tab  " link tools}
  @{"  The Sub-agents tab  " link subagents}
  @{"  How saving works  " link saving}
  @{"  What needs a restart  " link restart}

For developers
  @{"  The HTTP API  " link api}
  @{"  Limits and known gaps  " link limits}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The panel: list and head"
@{h1}The panel: list and head

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents & tools@{ub}, "Agent Editor"). It
needs authentication switched on and an active admin account; with
authentication off it says so and shows nothing else.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Agent Editor"}

@{h2}The list

On the left, every agent of the config tree, grouped by where it is
defined: @{tt}config@{utt} for everything outside the plugin folders, the
plugin's name for a file under @{tt}src/plugins*/<plugin>/@{utt}. The
search looks at names, descriptions and tags; the filter shows all agents,
the enabled or disabled ones, those with problems, those that need a
restart, or the read-only ones. The corner of the list sets its width,
which the panel keeps for the next visit. By default nothing reloads by
itself: the reload button loads anew, and the menu next to it turns on an
automatic reload (every 30 seconds unless you pick another interval),
skipped while a write is on its way; a form with unsaved changes is left as it is.

Each row shows the description and these badges:

@{table}
Badge | Means
off | the entry is disabled
new | enabled on disk, not started yet
restart | the running app has older settings; see @{" What needs a restart " link restart}
read-only | the panel cannot write this entry; the reason is in the badge's tooltip
N problems | what the editor warns about; the problems are in the tooltip
@{body}

An entry is an agent when the running app says so for it. For an entry
the app has not declared, its final type decides: @{tt}agent@{utt}, a
plugin type that builds agents lazily, or a type all of whose running
instances are agents.

The problems are read from the files as saved: "Does not resolve"
(with the loader's reason), "Unknown model profile", "Prompt template not
found", "Unknown skill", "Matches no tool" (an allowed or blocked pattern
that names no tool of the running app), "Deferred matches no allowed tool"
(a @{tt}tools.deferred@{utt} pattern that holds back none of the agent's
tools) and "External server is off" (an
allowed pattern for an external MCP server that is disabled). When the
config defines no model profiles at all, every profile of a chain is
unknown. When the tool catalogue cannot be built, the patterns are not
checked and a banner above the list says so; so does any file the loader
cannot read.

@{h2}New agent

@{b}New@{ub} asks for a name -- lower case letters, digits and
@{tt}_@{utt}, starting with a letter, 2 to 64 characters -- and what to
start from:

@{bullet}@{b}Blank basic_agent@{ub}: @{tt}type: basic_agent@{utt},
enabled.
@{bullet}@{b}Inherit from an agent@{ub}: @{tt}type@{utt} is that agent;
everything else comes from it.
@{bullet}@{b}Copy of an agent@{ub}: its own entry, copied.

The form then opens with the new agent, marked "new, not saved yet";
nothing is written before @{b}Save@{ub}. The file is
@{tt}config/agents/<name>.yaml@{utt}; see @{" How saving works " link saving}.

@{h2}The head

Above the tabs: the agent's name; what it is based on (a button that
opens the parent agent, or the plugin class); its file, with a button that
copies the path; and how it relates to the running app:

@{table}
Shown | Means
Active | the app runs it with the settings on disk (ready, not necessarily busy)
Active with older settings | the app runs it, the files say something else
Not active yet | enabled on disk, started with the next restart
Disabled | disabled and not running
Still active, gone from disk | running, but disabled or removed in the files
@{body}

A banner under the head says what a restart or a config reload would apply
-- with a @{b}Reload config@{ub} button when a reload applies something --
and why a read-only agent cannot be edited.

@{b}Revert@{ub} goes back to the entry as loaded; "unsaved changes" marks
an edited form. @{b}Save@{ub} shows the diff and writes on confirmation.
The menu next to it offers @{b}Duplicate@{ub} (a copy under a new name),
@{b}New child agent@{ub} (one that inherits from this one) and
@{b}Delete@{ub}, which is off while another entry inherits from this one
(its tooltip names them). Leaving an agent, or opening another one, with
unsaved changes asks first.

@{b}Reload config@{ub} in the toolbar asks, then calls the core's
@{tt}POST /admin/reload-config@{utt} and reports how many servers it
refreshed or which ones failed.

@{h2}When the server refuses

A refused write shows its reason; nothing was written. When the file changed
on disk since the agent was loaded, a dialog offers @{b}Keep my
changes@{ub} (the form stays, a save is refused until you reload) or
@{b}Reload from disk@{ub}.
@endnode

@node tabs "The tabs"
@{h1}The tabs

A field the entry does not set shows the inherited value, marked
@{i}inherited@{ui}. Setting it writes the key into the entry; the reset
button next to its label removes the key again. The Tools and Sub-agents
tabs have nodes of their own: @{" The Tools tab " link tools},
@{" The Sub-agents tab " link subagents}.

@{h2}General

@{bullet}@{b}Enabled@{ub} -- whether the app starts the agent. Never
inherited: an entry without it stays off.
@{bullet}@{b}Based on@{ub} -- an agent class (starts from its defaults) or
another agent (passes on everything it has). Changing it reloads the
inherited values of every tab.
@{bullet}@{b}Description@{ub}, @{b}Visibility@{ub} (ui, tool, both,
private -- the help line says what each means), @{b}Category@{ub},
@{b}Tags@{ub} (Enter adds one).

@{h2}Model

@{bullet}@{b}Model chain@{ub} -- profiles in order: the first answers,
the others take over when it fails. Add one by name, provider or model;
move and remove with the buttons. An unknown profile is marked. Emptied,
the key goes and the inherited chain applies (the loader refuses an empty
one).
@{bullet}@{b}Advanced chain@{ub} -- used while the agent escalates.
Without it @{tt}use_advanced_model@{utt} runs on the model chain and
auto-escalation stays off. Emptied while the parent has one, it is
written as an empty list, which switches the inherited one off.
@{bullet}@{b}Caller's model@{ub} (@{tt}inherit_parent_llm@{utt}) -- run
on the model the calling run was switched to, when it was switched.
@{bullet}@{b}Fallback recovery@{ub} -- seconds a failed profile rests.
@{bullet}@{b}LLM parameters@{ub} -- YAML, for every profile or keyed by
profile name or @{tt}*@{utt}.

@{h2}Run

The core's run settings, key by key: @{b}Max steps@{ub}; auto-escalation
(@{tt}auto_escalate_on_stuck@{utt}, @{tt}escalate_error_streak@{utt},
@{tt}escalate_rounds@{utt}, @{tt}escalate_max_calls@{utt}); loop detection
(@{tt}enabled@{utt}, @{tt}history_size@{utt},
@{tt}exact_match_threshold@{utt}, @{tt}sequence_threshold@{utt},
@{tt}block_after_threshold@{utt}, @{tt}auto_unblock_after_steps@{utt});
reasoning loop detection (@{tt}enabled@{utt},
@{tt}repetition_threshold@{utt} from 0.01 to 1); and the timeouts
@{tt}session_lock_timeout@{utt}, @{tt}tool_cleanup_timeout@{utt},
@{tt}llm_task_max_iterations@{utt}. Each field has a help line.

A number outside what the runtime can use -- the config would take it --
is marked red, counts as an unsaved change and holds @{b}Save@{ub} back.
A note above the escalation fields says when auto-escalation cannot run:
no advanced chain, one that starts with the model chain's first profile,
advanced steps or calls at 0, or chains inherited from a parent that does
not resolve.

@{h2}Prompt

@{bullet}@{b}Template file@{ub} or @{b}Inline prompt@{ub}. A template
path starting with @{tt}./@{utt} or @{tt}../@{utt} is relative to the
agent's file, any other to the repository root. The preview shows the file
and whether it is found. An inline prompt wins over a template; under a
parent with an inline prompt, choosing a template file writes an empty
@{tt}system_prompt@{utt} so the file applies.
@{bullet}@{b}Template variables@{ub} -- YAML, the prompt's Jinja variables.
@{bullet}@{b}Skills@{ub} -- two checklists, @{b}Always@{ub} (in the prompt
from the start) and @{b}On demand@{ub} (listed; the agent loads them),
with a search and a filter. A bare list (@{tt}skills: [a]@{utt}) is the
"always" list; changing one of the two writes it out as
@{tt}always:@{utt}, and "on demand" stays inherited. A list with
@{tt}+@{utt} or @{tt}!@{utt} entries is edited in the YAML tab.

@{h2}Hooks

A switch for all lifecycle hooks of the agent, and a table of the hooks the
app has registered, one row per hook name, with its types and default. Per
hook: inherited/default, on or off for this agent, and its own settings as
YAML (the gear button). They are written to
@{tt}agent_config.hooks.overrides.<hook>@{utt}. Search, type and state
filters narrow the table; overrides for hooks the app does not know are
listed below it.

@{h2}YAML

The agent's own entry as it is written to the file, for every key without
a form control (@{tt}self_tool_descriptions@{utt}, a plugin's own keys).
@{b}Apply to form@{ub} takes the typed text; so does leaving the tab. Text
that does not parse stays, with the error below it, and keeps the tab
open. @{b}Show the form's entry@{ub} drops the typed text.

@{h2}Every YAML field

An error under a YAML field is about the text it was shown for: typing on
hides it, leaving the field or saving parses again. A save stopped by YAML
that does not parse opens the tab and the field. Anchors, aliases, dates
and keys that are not text are refused: quote such values. An unquoted
@{tt}- !server/*@{utt} is read as the removal entry it looks like and
written back quoted.
@endnode

@node tools "The Tools tab"
@{h1}The Tools tab

The tab's title counts the tools that reach the agent. At the top, a live
summary: how many tools reach it, how many tools each pattern names, and
which patterns name nothing ("matches nothing"). A pattern for an external
MCP server is not judged -- the editor has no list of external tools -- and
is marked "external, not checked", or "external server off".

@{h2}Allowed and Blocked

Two lists, each with a source:

@{table}
Source | Means
Inherited | the parent's list, unchanged
Extend inherited | the inherited entries stay; this agent adds (@{tt}+x@{utt}) or removes (@{tt}!x@{utt}) entries
Own list | this list replaces the inherited one
@{body}

A list that mixes @{tt}+@{utt}/@{tt}!@{utt} entries with plain ones is
refused by the loader; the tab says so and edits nothing until a source is
picked. Switching from an own list to "extend" turns what it had beyond
the inherited list into @{tt}+@{utt} entries and what it dropped into
@{tt}!@{utt} entries.

@{h2}The tree

Every tool server of the running app with its tools, searchable (server
names, plugin types, tool names and descriptions) and filtered to the
servers in the list or not in it. Ticking a server writes
@{tt}server/*@{utt}; ticking a tool writes @{tt}server/tool@{utt}.
A tool that another pattern grants or blocks is locked, and its label
names the pattern; a blocked tool shows unticked in the allowed tree.
Patterns that are not a server or a tool of the tree are chips below it;
type one and press Enter.

@{h2}How patterns match

The panel counts as the agent does. A server is considered when the
allowed list as a whole matches it (a @{tt}server/tool@{utt} entry counts);
its tools are kept when the list matches them; then the blocked ones are
dropped. So @{tt}web/web_*@{utt} alone grants nothing -- the server pass
does not let @{tt}web@{utt} through -- but next to @{tt}web/web_search@{utt}
it grants every @{tt}web_*@{utt} tool. An external server's tool is
reached by its exact dotted name (@{tt}everything.echo@{utt}) or a pattern
ending in @{tt}*@{utt} (@{tt}everything.*@{utt}); a bare
@{tt}everything@{utt} or @{tt}everything/*@{utt} is not.
@endnode

@node subagents "The Sub-agents tab"
@{h1}The Sub-agents tab

Only there when the @{tt}sub_agent_manager@{utt} plugin is installed. An
agent starts sub-agents through the managers its allowed tools grant, and
each manager's own lists decide which agents it can start.

@{h2}Managers this agent uses

One row per enabled manager: a switch for the grant, which agents it can
start, whether it runs yet ("not active yet"), and @{b}Configure@{ub}.

@{bullet}The switch writes @{tt}<manager>/*@{utt} into the agent's allowed
tools (or @{tt}!<manager>/*@{utt} to take back an inherited grant); it is
saved with the agent. It is locked, the reason in its tooltip, while a
pattern it does not write grants the manager or a block stops it -- edit
those in the Tools tab.
@{bullet}@{b}Configure@{ub} opens the manager's own entry: the agents it
can start (a checklist with search and filter) and its limits
(@{tt}max_sub_agents_per_session@{utt}, @{tt}max_sub_agents_per_type@{utt},
@{tt}max_nesting_depth@{utt}, @{tt}default_wait_timeout@{utt}: whole
numbers from 0, empty for the plugin default;
@{tt}auto_archive_on_limit@{utt}, @{tt}allow_advanced_model@{utt}). A list
with patterns or @{tt}+@{utt}/@{tt}!@{utt} entries, or none, is shown as
it is; @{b}Pick agents instead@{ub} replaces it with a checklist.
@{b}Save manager@{ub} writes the manager's entry after its own diff; its
other keys stay. Unsaved manager edits are asked about before they are
dropped.
@{bullet}@{b}New manager@{ub} creates @{tt}config/agents/<name>.yaml@{utt}
with a manager that starts no agent yet, grants it to this agent and opens
it. It runs after the next restart.

@{h2}Managers that may start this agent

One switch per manager, with the list entry that decides (blocked,
@{tt}*@{utt}, the exact name, a pattern). It is written into that
manager's file right away, after its diff, and edits the manager's lists
as little as possible:

@{bullet}A list the manager does not have, or has with
@{tt}+@{utt}/@{tt}!@{utt} entries, gets @{tt}+name@{utt} (or
@{tt}!name@{utt} to lift an inherited block), so the inherited list keeps
applying; a plain list gets the bare name.
@{bullet}Allowing takes the agent off @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt} and, if
that is not enough, adds it to @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}; stopping takes it
off @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} and, if still allowed, adds it to
@{tt}blocked_agents@{utt}.
@{bullet}A @{tt}+@{utt}/@{tt}!@{utt} list that becomes empty is removed,
not left as @{tt}[]@{utt}, which would replace the inherited one.
@{bullet}A manager whose own list is a single value (@{tt}"*"@{utt}) is not
changed; the answer says so.
@endnode

@node saving "How saving works"
@{h1}How saving works

@{h2}Only the own entry

The editor edits the agent's entry exactly as written in its file:
@{tt}${NAME}@{utt} stays unexpanded, @{tt}+@{utt}/@{tt}!@{utt} prefixes
and @{tt}./@{utt} paths stay. Inherited values are never copied in.

@{h2}The steps of a save

@{bullet}@{b}Diff first.@{ub} The panel asks for a dry run and shows the
change; nothing is written until @{b}Write file@{ub}. A save that changes
nothing says so.
@{bullet}@{b}Conflict check.@{ub} The panel sends the version of the file
it loaded (a hash of its bytes). When the file changed on disk meanwhile,
the save is refused ("changed on disk; reload"). The file is read once
more right before the write.
@{bullet}@{b}In place.@{ub} Only the lines the edit changes are
rendered anew; every other line is copied byte for byte, CRLF files stay
CRLF. Removed keys go, new keys go to the end of their block, a changed
list keeps its flow style and the comments of the items it still holds.
Values are written so the loader reads them back unchanged
(@{tt}1e-05@{utt} as @{tt}1.0e-05@{utt}; @{tt}on@{utt}, @{tt}yes@{utt},
@{tt}null@{utt}, @{tt}y@{utt} quoted). The result must parse back to
exactly the intended data, else nothing is written and the answer names
the value, or says the file's layout does not allow an in-place edit.
@{bullet}@{b}Atomic write.@{ub} A temporary file beside it, then a
rename; the file keeps its permissions.
@{bullet}@{b}Check and roll back.@{ub} The whole config is loaded again;
the agent and every agent that inherits from it are checked: a type chain
that ends in an unknown type, an inheritance cycle, the inheritance merge,
every profile of both chains, the effective template (unless an inline
prompt wins). A problem that was not there before restores the old file
and answers with one @{tt}location: message@{utt} per line. Problems an
entry already had neither block a save nor hide a new one. If the file
changed again during the check, it is left alone and the answer says so;
if the old content cannot be put back, the answer names the file that
holds the rejected content.

@{h2}New agents

A new agent is written to @{tt}config/agents/<name>.yaml@{utt} with the
header comment "Created with the Agent Editor.". The name must not be a
server, a plugin type or an existing file; the file is only created, never
replaced, and only inside the repository. A copy re-bases its
@{tt}./@{utt} and @{tt}../@{utt} template paths to the new file. When the
config's includes do not pick the file up, or the agent does not load, the
file is removed again.

@{h2}Delete

Refused while another entry inherits from the agent. The entry goes with
the comment block directly above its key (at the key's own indentation),
the comments inside it and one separating blank line. When it was the
file's last server, the file keeps @{tt}servers: {}@{utt}; it is deleted
only when nothing but blank lines, that structure and the editor's own
header would remain. The dialog names managers whose own
@{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} still list the agent.

@{h2}Secrets and paths in what the panel shows

Inherited and effective values are what the loader made of the files,
with two things put back. A value the config expanded from
@{tt}${NAME}@{utt} shows as @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} -- for every variable a
config file names and every name in the secrets files; a value of 8
characters or more is replaced wherever it appears, a shorter one only
where it is the whole string. Error texts show such values of 8
characters or more as @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} as well. And a
template path the loader made absolute shows relative to the repository,
so a value copied into an entry stays valid.
@endnode

@node restart "What needs a restart"
@{h1}What needs a restart

The app builds its agents when it starts. The panel compares each
resolved entry on disk with what the app runs -- the whole entry except
@{tt}enabled@{utt}; for a built agent its live @{tt}agent_config@{utt} and
role gate, which a config reload changes.

@{table}
State | Means
in_sync | disk and app agree ("Active")
changed | they differ; the keys are named ("Active with older settings")
new | enabled on disk, not started ("Not active yet")
removed | started, but disabled or gone on disk ("Still active, gone from disk")
off | disabled and not started ("Disabled")
@{body}

Changed keys are named one level down where it helps:
@{tt}max_steps@{utt}, @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt},
@{tt}hooks.overrides@{utt}, @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt}.

@{b}Reload config@{ub} applies only @{tt}max_steps@{utt},
@{tt}auto_escalate_on_stuck@{utt}, @{tt}escalate_rounds@{utt},
@{tt}escalate_max_calls@{utt}, @{tt}escalate_error_streak@{utt},
@{tt}fallback_recovery_seconds@{utt}, @{tt}inherit_parent_llm@{utt} and
@{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}, and only to agents that are built and
registered as plugin servers; the panel lists those separately. Everything
else -- model chain, tools, prompt, hooks, visibility, any change to an
agent not built yet -- takes a restart. The panel only says so: restarting
is the operator's.
@endnode

@node api "The HTTP API"
@{h1}The HTTP API

All under @{tt}/plugins/agent_editor/@{utt}, JSON in and out; an error's
@{tt}detail@{utt} is a string. Every data endpoint, reads included, checks for
an active admin in the user database itself, on top of the route rules: a
token's role claim does not count, API keys are refused. Writes take
@{tt}Content-Type: application/json@{utt} only.

@{table}
Method and path | Does
GET / | the panel
GET /agents | every agent row (file, group, editable, state, restart, problems) and errors
GET /agents/{name} | own, inherited and effective entry, parent, children, version, live state, sub-agent access, prompt file
GET /inherited?type= | what an entry of that type gets without keys of its own; without type, basic_agent
POST /agents | create: name, entry, source (a copy), dry_run
PUT /agents/{name} | save: entry, version, dry_run
DELETE /agents/{name} | delete: version, dry_run
GET /managers | the enabled sub-agent managers with their lists and the agents each can start
POST /managers | create a manager: name, allowed_agents, dry_run
PUT /managers/{name} | save a manager's entry; its type and enabled cannot change here
PUT /agents/{name}/spawnable | sam, allowed, version (of the manager's file), dry_run
GET /meta | picker data: agent classes, profiles, skills, hooks, prompt files, reloadable fields
GET /tools | the tools of every tool server, by server
POST /tools/effective | the tools a pair of lists yields after inheritance, with per-pattern counts
GET /prompt?path=&agent= | a prompt file; ./ paths relative to the agent's file
POST /yaml | an entry rendered as YAML
POST /yaml/parse | YAML text parsed into an entry
@{body}

A write with @{tt}dry_run@{utt} answers the diff only; without it the
answer carries the file's new @{tt}version@{utt}.

@{table}
Status | When
400 | the write is not possible: a read-only entry, a name taken, an entry still inherited from, a manager list that cannot change that way, a value or layout that cannot be written in place, a refused prompt path
401 | not signed in
403 | not an active admin, or authentication off
404 | unknown agent, type or manager
409 | the file changed on disk (the detail says so)
415 | not JSON
422 | an invalid request, or an entry that does not load (the file is back as it was)
500 | the config on disk does not load, or the old content could not be put back
@{body}

The prompt preview reads only relative paths to @{tt}.md@{utt},
@{tt}.txt@{utt} and @{tt}.markdown@{utt} files inside the repository, at
most 200 000 characters. Absolute, UNC, drive and stream paths are refused
as text before the path is touched.
@endnode

@node limits "Limits and known gaps"
@{h1}Limits and known gaps

@{bullet}@{b}Read-only entries.@{ub} An entry defined in more than one
file is shown but not editable, and so is one in a read-only file, a file
outside the repository, a file mixing CRLF and LF line endings, or a file
the round-trip parser cannot read (a duplicate key, which the loader
accepts). An entry holding a value JSON cannot carry (a date, a number as
a key, @{tt}.inf@{utt}) shows it as text and cannot be saved or copied
from the form; deleting it and the manager switches still work.
@{bullet}@{b}Rare layouts are refused.@{ub} When the in-place result
would not read back as intended -- irregularly indented keys, a file that
ends inside a block scalar without a final newline -- nothing is written.
@{bullet}@{b}Comments in a replaced list.@{ub} A list of mappings that
changes loses the comments inside the mappings it replaces; a list written
as @{tt}[]@{utt} becomes a block list with its first item.
@{bullet}@{b}Rollback window.@{ub} Between the write and the check the
new file is on disk for about a tenth of a second.
@{bullet}@{b}Tool catalogue.@{ub} Only servers the app has built: an agent
not started yet (lazy start) and external MCP servers are not in the tree.
@{bullet}@{b}Short secrets.@{ub} A secret shorter than 8 characters is
masked only where it is a whole value.
@{bullet}@{b}Others editing.@{ub} The editor reads the files again at most
once a second; the panel shows another edit at its next refresh, and the
version check keeps a save from overwriting it.
@{bullet}@{b}Without a runtime.@{ub} In an app without a runtime in its
state, every live state is empty.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    agent_editor:
      type: agent_editor
      enabled: true
@{body}

It needs @{tt}auth.enabled: true@{utt} and an admin account. The route
rule in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt} keeps non-admins out before the
plugin's own check:

@{code yaml}
- pattern: "/plugins/agent_editor/*"
  policy: "require_auth"
  min_role: "admin"
@{body}

New agents and managers are written to @{tt}config/agents/<name>.yaml@{utt};
the master config must include that folder (the default includes
@{tt}agents*/*.yaml@{utt}).

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
config_path | the app's config, else config/config.yaml | the master config of the tree to edit
root | the working directory | the repository root: files outside it are read-only, templates resolve against it
@{body}

Both are for tests; the running app needs neither. The plugin depends on
@{tt}ruamel.yaml@{utt}, which renders a file keeping its comments. No data
of its own: it reads and writes the config files.
@endnode
